---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import CardVisual from '../../components/CardVisual.astro';

const steps = [
  { n: '1', t: 'Compress', d: 'Train an encoder–decoder that maps a patient’s full risk-factor profile to a latent state and back.' },
  { n: '2', t: 'Learn to complete', d: 'Train a JEPA-style predictor to infer the full latent state from a profile with some features masked out.' },
  { n: '3', t: 'Edit one factor', d: 'DiCE proposes a change (e.g. lower BMI). Keep the edited features, mask the ones that should respond, and let the predictor complete the latent state.' },
  { n: '4', t: 'Decode & re-score', d: 'Decode a full, internally consistent profile, re-run the risk model, and attribute the change in risk to each input.' },
];
---
<Base title="Correlation-aware counterfactuals" description="Making counterfactual lifestyle recommendations from a dementia risk model respect correlations between risk factors.">
  <ProjectHeader
    title="Correlation-aware counterfactuals"
    subtitle="Making “what should I change?” advice from a dementia risk model physiologically plausible."
    status="Early stage · in progress"
    role="Research assistant (continuing a project from a graduated student)"
    period="2026 – present"
    tags={['Counterfactual explanations', 'Representation learning', 'Tabular health data', 'APOE ε4']}
    tldr={[
      'A dementia risk calculator is most useful when it tells people <strong>what to change</strong>. Counterfactual tools such as DiCE search for the smallest input change that lowers predicted risk.',
      'DiCE edits features <strong>independently</strong>, but lowering BMI usually also moves blood pressure and activity level. Edits that ignore this can be implausible.',
      'Plan: learn the joint structure of risk factors in a latent space and let it <strong>fill in the correlated changes</strong> before re-scoring risk.',
    ]}
  />

  <figure class="dv panel"><CardVisual name="dementia" /><figcaption>Idea sketch: an edit to one factor propagates to correlated factors before risk is recomputed.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Current step</h2>
  <p>
    I am measuring how the model’s input features co-vary, including BMI, blood pressure and physical activity, to see where
    independent edits break realistic structure and which features the completion model must handle.
  </p>

  <h2>Planned method</h2>
  <ol class="plan">
    {steps.map((s) => (
      <li><span class="n">{s.n}</span><div><strong>{s.t}.</strong> {s.d}</div></li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p>
    The goal is a per-feature risk attribution computed on <em>realistic</em> counterfactual profiles, which should give more accurate and more
    actionable lifestyle suggestions than independent feature edits.
  </p>

  <p class="muted small">Work in progress. Methods and results will be added as they mature.</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .dv { max-width: 520px; }
  .plan { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem; display: grid; gap: 10px; counter-reset: none; }
  .plan li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 34px 1fr; gap: 12px; align-items: start; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 12px 14px; margin: 0; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: .95rem; }
  .plan strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .n { display: grid; place-items: center; width: 28px; height: 28px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent-ink); font-weight: 600; font-size: .85rem; }
</style>
